# Windows script hosts are line-ending sensitive. cmd.exe mis-parses a .bat
# with LF-only endings -- a multi-line `if (...)` block silently stops working,
# which is exactly how run.bat shipped broken: it launched nothing at all while
# run.vbs (wscript tolerates LF) kept working, so the breakage looked like
# "the batch file is just slow".
# Forcing eol=crlf here means git hands out a WORKING launcher on every clone,
# regardless of the cloner's core.autocrlf setting.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.vbs   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Everything else: normalize in the repo, let git pick the checkout ending.
*.py    text
*.js    text
*.css   text
*.html  text
*.md    text
*.toml  text
*.yml   text
*.json  text
*.txt   text

# Authority receipts are immutable audit evidence pinned by content digests
# (docs/saipen-persistence.md, "Authority receipts"); the engine verifies them
# byte-exactly (verify_integrity / release_scope_matches). They must survive a
# fresh clone byte-for-byte, so eol handling is DISABLED for them - text
# normalization would rewrite LF to CRLF on a Windows clone and break every
# digest pinned in the journal.
.saipen/intake/**             -text
.saipen/archive/source/**     -text
.saipen/kitchen/release_scope/** -text

# Binary -- never touch.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
